      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63017,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histiocytic nodules present as small pinkish-yellow nodules measuring &lt;&nbsp;1&nbsp;cm or as a solitary, filmy adhesion {22271884; 15185256}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:21:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63018,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Histiocytic nodules are characterized by a compact and nodular aggregate of histiocytes. Mesothelial cells may be intermixed. The histiocytes have a moderate amount of amphophilic, eosinophilic, or basophilic cytoplasm and oval or kidney-shaped nuclei that can show grooves. Fibrinous material is sometimes seen. By immunohistochemistry, histiocytes are highlighted by CD68, CD16, CD163, lysozyme, CD4, and CD64 {27340746; 22271884}, but unlike mesothelial cells, they do not stain for calretinin and CK5/6.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-19T16:07:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
